From: butthead fsck incident 2026-09-20
Subject: [PATCH] tools: raise userspace percpu thread cap 256 -> 8192

Userspace fsck on a large multi-device filesystem aborts with

    bch_percpu_thread_init: too many threads (max 256)
    bcachefs: fatal SIGABRT

mid-repair (observed twice in check_reconcile_work while fixing
stripe_needs_reconcile_not_set on a 10-device pool).

Every OS thread that touches fs code takes a PERMANENT slot via
bch_percpu_thread_init() - slots are never released on thread exit -
while workqueue worker pools in the userspace shim grow on demand up
to WQ_DFL_ACTIVE (256) per queue when work blocks. A repair run doing
thousands of transaction commits on slow devices grows flush/write
queues to hundreds of short-lived workers; 256 slots are exhausted and
fsck dies mid-pass, leaving the filesystem unclean.

The cap is only the size of the chunk-pointer registry plus the abort
check; chunks are 256MB NORESERVE anonymous mappings, i.e. address
space, not memory (pages fault in as touched). 8192 slots = 2TB of
reserved VA on 64-bit, well within the 128TB user address space, and
gives 32x headroom over the observed failure.

A proper fix (reusing slots of exited threads, with care for stale
percpu counters and lock-free readers of the chunk registry) is left
for upstream; this unblocks long userspace repair runs.

Reproduced on bcachefs-tools 1.39.6, 32-core machine, 10-device pool:
two consecutive offline fsck runs died after ~6.1k and ~4.2k committed
repairs respectively.

Signed-off-by: z-247
---
 include/linux/percpu.h | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/include/linux/percpu.h b/include/linux/percpu.h
--- a/include/linux/percpu.h
+++ b/include/linux/percpu.h
@@ -40,7 +40,17 @@ extern char __start_bch_percpu[], __stop_bch_percpu[];
 
 #define DECLARE_PER_CPU(type, name)	extern type name
 
-#define BCH_PERCPU_MAX_CPUS	256
+/*
+ * Slots are never released: every thread that calls
+ * bch_percpu_thread_init() holds its slot until process exit, and
+ * userspace workqueue pools spawn fresh short-lived workers under
+ * blocking load (up to WQ_DFL_ACTIVE per queue). Long repair runs
+ * (offline fsck with thousands of fixes on slow devices) exhaust 256
+ * slots and abort mid-pass. Chunks are NORESERVE address space, not
+ * memory - be generous; 8192 x 256MB = 2TB reserved VA, fine on
+ * 64-bit.
+ */
+#define BCH_PERCPU_MAX_CPUS	8192
 
 /*
  * Per-thread chunk layout: [static_section][dynamic_arena].
